Z||000000||RobertVol||04-21-2000||02:14 AM||||Fir you guys that have t tops out there. Have you had any probles with the plastic panels creaking and giving alot mote than usual. The noise over every single bump is driving me CRAZY. Is there any Fabrications that out great Fiero experts have done to tighten these panels up to stop the noises. I have tried tightnening the screw back down at the top of the a pillar and around the t tops area but still BADLY creaking. A little flex is one thing but MAN ! anyway if you have any suggestions let me know...||||reg||1
Z||000001||Raydar||04-21-2000||01:30 PM||||Are you sure it's not the glass roof panels that are making the noise? The latching pins can be adjusted to pull the glass down, tighter to the seals. There are two screws, very close to where the pin exits the mechanism. If you loosen the screws, the position of the pin can be adjusted closer to the glass. You will have to take off the plastic housing that covers the mechanism.<BR>Mine used to rattle like he!! before I did this. <P>Check this link... <A HREF="http://scooter.fierottop.com/waterleak/leak6.html" TARGET=_blank>http://scooter.fierottop.com/waterleak/leak6.html</A>  <P>This is the "front page" of the above... <A HREF="http://www.fierottop.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fierottop.com</A> <P>This is the Fiero T-Top Registry. Lots of good stuff.<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 04-21-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||RobertVol||04-21-2000||02:56 PM||||Your right about that. Some it is the t tops. thanks for the link. <P>there is still some in the "a" pillar panel and the screws to my interior area are stripped. I would like to replace some of these areas. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||bHooper||04-24-2000||10:34 AM||||there used to be a material called "Dynamat", it is a sound deadening material.  it was available from high end car stereo stores.  this material efectively "kills" the substance that it is attached too... removeing rattles and increasing the effectiveness of your stereo system.  if you can find this, it can be cut into strips and bonded to the underside of your panel's with a heat gun.  <P>without a doubt, this will fix the problem!<P>i found a link, check it out... <A HREF="http://www.dynamat.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.dynamat.com/</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||bHooper||04-24-2000||10:53 AM||||not to be a pest, but i found it for sale at crutchfield.com  it is a little pricey, but a little bit goes a long way... yoiu do not have to apply it to the entire molding (unless you want too) you can cut it into strips and place it in high noise locations (i.e.: between the clips)                                         <BR>Dynamat 400 Super, 4 Sq. ft.   One 18" x 32" Sheet  $34.95                               <BR>Dynamat 10145 Original   Bulk, 13 Sq. Ft., 1 Sheet  $69.95  <P>Dynamat 10115 Original   Speaker Kit, 10-in. x 10-in. 2 Sheets  $14.95  <P>Dynamat 10155 Original   Bulk, 39 Sq. Ft., 3 Sheets $199.95  <P>Dynamat 10125 Original   Custom Kit, 4 Sq. Ft., 1 Sheet  $24.95  <P>Dynamat 10245 Premium   Bulk, 13 Sq. Ft., 1 Sheet $79.95  <P>Dynamat 10345 Super   Bulk 12 Sq. Ft., 1 Sheet  $89.95  <BR>                                                                                                                       ||||reg||1
Z||000005||88formula||04-24-2000||07:59 PM||||I have used Dynomat and it works OK. One day bought a similar generic material from JC Whitney. It cost about a quarter of the price and it looked, smelled and felt exactly like Dynomat but it did not have the Dynomat lettering on it. I think the same company made this stuff. I put this stuff in my Impala and did notice that the interior was a little more hushed especially when I drove over course pavement, but I covered the whole floor pan and pieced the sides and doors.||||reg||1
Z||000006||bHooper||04-24-2000||10:01 PM||||thats interesting about the "generic material".  do you remember what it was called?  or do you have a part number?<P>the one thing you have to remember about a product such as dynamat... it is not designed to eliminate road noise, although it is somewhat efective at that... it is designed to "kill" body panels.  by that i mean, it should kill any resonance or rattle in the panel.  it is designed to compliment high end car stereo systems by absorbing wasted energy.  <P>unfortunately i forget what there exact claims were, something like: 1 layer = an effective 5db gain from your amp, 2 layers =12db, ect.<P>because the material is good at "killing" the resonance in the body panel, it is excellent for rattles produce by molding.  <P>but haveing said this, if you can get a similar product for .25 of the price and it works... that is the way to go.<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000007||Raydar||04-24-2000||10:57 PM||||I have also heard (not verified) that the building supply stores (Home Depot, and such) carry rubber roofing membrane that will have the same effect. This is the rubber that they put on the top of office buildings. Also waaayyyy cheaper than Dynamat. ||||reg||1
